15:34:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because from a business perspective, fixing it costs real money and delivers zero measurable return. The hold music experience isn’t broken in a way that hurts their bottom line enough to matter. Here’s the cold corporate math: **1. Hold music is a cost, not a feature.** Nobody chooses a bank, an airline, or an insurance company because their hold music was crisp and hi-fi. They choose based on price, coverage, or convenience. A company’s phone system is a support cost center—every dollar spent on it is a dollar not spent on marketing or product. Making on-hold audio sound better would require upgrading PBX hardware, SIP trunk configurations, licensing wideband codecs, re-encoding audio files, and sometimes replacing the whole on-hold player system. That’s a multi-thousand-dollar project that shows up exactly nowhere in customer satisfaction surveys (because everyone expects it to sound awful anyway). The ROI simply isn’t there. **2. The pain isn’t unique—it’s universal.** If one airline had crystal-clear hold music and all others sounded like a tin can, that airline might actually get a slight competitive nudge. But since *every* company’s hold music is equally terrible, no one is being punished for it. The bar is set at rock bottom, and the market doesn’t reward raising it for something that isn’t a core service. **3. The vast majority of calls still touch analog or narrowband paths.** Even if a business went all-in on HD Voice internally, their SIP trunk provider connects to the public PSTN, and as soon as a call comes from a landline, a non-VoLTE mobile, or any legacy interconnect, the whole session drops to narrowband. They can’t control the end-to-end chain. So any investment they make gets sabotaged for the majority of callers, making it a wasted effort. **4. It’s a coordination nightmare.** The “hold music” audio is often fed into the phone system by a third-party on-hold player (think those little USB sticks that plug into a PBX), or it’s a file uploaded by an office manager a decade ago. The IT team that manages the phone system often isn’t the same team that handles the audio, and no one has “check the fidelity of the hold music” on their quarterly review. It’s an orphaned responsibility. **5. There’s a hidden psychological nudge (whether intentional or not).** Some companies actually *like* that it sounds bad and is slightly irritating, because it discourages people from waiting on hold forever. If the music were a pleasant, hi-fi concert, customers might be more inclined to hang on longer, increasing the queue length and the company’s staffing costs. A tinny, grating loop makes you more likely to hang up or choose a self-service option—reducing their support load. I’m not saying they engineer it that way deliberately, but if it accidentally works in their favor, there’s no pressure to change. **6. The “fix” doesn’t solve the real problem: the hold itself.** Companies that actually care about customer experience don’t invest in better hold music; they invest in reducing hold time (call-back systems, better IVR, more agents). Fixing the audio quality of a 10-minute wait is like putting a mint on a dirty pillow instead of cleaning the room. It’s the wrong thing to optimize. So in short, you’re hearing the sound of corporate indifference. The cost to fix it is real, the benefit is imaginary, the technical hurdles are immense, and no one ever lost a customer because “the violins sounded too compressed.” It’s baked in, and it’s staying that way until holding itself becomes obsolete—not the audio quality of it.
